        "body": "### The feature, motivation and pitch\n\nDo you have plans to integrate a non-convex hull collision detection algorithm as the object collision detection method in the future?\n\nWe are currently conducting research on robot welding simulation. After selecting an edge on the workpiece in the scene, we generate the corresponding welding path, and then the robot with the welding torch follows the planned path.\n\nAt present, we use MuJoCo for visualization and collision detection.\n\nIn the early stage, when using MuJoCo to detect collisions between the welding torch and the workpiece, we found that even when the torch was clearly inside a cavity of the workpiece with no physical interference, MuJoCo still reported a collision. After investigating, we learned that MuJoCo uses convex hulls for collision detection between objects (you can press H in simulate.exe to view the convex hulls of models), which cannot meet our need for precise collision detection.\n\nWe then looked into convex decomposition, but even after using CoACD for decomposition, the results were still unsatisfactory (because our workpiece model is too complex).\n\nNext, we discovered that MuJoCo supports SDF. When we set the collision model of the workpiece to SDF in the MJCF file, the results still fell short (and when the `sdf_iterations` and `sdf_initpoints` parameters were set too high, the program would exit directly with a `too many contact points` error).\n\nFinally, we basically solved the problem by integrating [Coal](https://github.com/coal-library/coal) into MuJoCo. For the specific integration approach, see [[QMuJoCoScene](https://github.com/G-Yong/QMuJoCoScene)].\n\nWe also conducted a simulation of a small ball sliding down a chute to the ground, which worked reasonably well.\n\n[assets/coalCollision.mkv](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/49a18818-608c-457d-b89a-a16b8b40a035)\n\n### Alternatives\n\n Integrate  [coal](https://github.com/coal-library/coal) into `mjCOLLISIONFUNC`\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
